window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"21-09","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"21-09-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-01/","title_he":"א – כבוד ספר תורה","title_en":"1. Honouring the Torah Scroll","body_en":"<p>Towards his death, Moshe Rabbeinu wrote the whole Torah in one book (Gittin 60a), and instructed the priests who carried the Ark of the Covenant of the Lord to place the book in the Holy of Holies beside the Ark of the Covenant as a testimony. As it is said (Devarim 31:24-26): \"And it came to pass, when Moshe had finished writing the words of this Torah in a book, until they were finished, that Moshe commanded the Levites who carried the Ark of the Covenant of the Lord, saying: Take this book of the Torah, and put it by the side of the Ark of the Covenant of the Lord your God, that it may be there for a witness against you.\" Some say that the Torah was placed inside the Ark, and some say beside it (Bava Batra 14b). The Ark of the Covenant, which held the Tablets of the Covenant, expressed the covenant that God made with His people Israel, and since it was on the basis of the covenant God made with Israel that He gave them His Torah, the Torah scroll was placed beside it. And this is also the order of the levels – first God chose Israel to be His treasured people, and afterwards He gave Israel His Torah. As in the wording of the blessing of the Torah: \"who chose us from all the peoples\", and from this \"gave us His Torah\".</p>\n<p>It follows that the Torah scroll written in ink on parchment, according to the tradition of its giving at Sinai, is the faithful testimony to Israel and to all the inhabitants of the world that God chose us from all the peoples and gave us His Torah. Therefore Israel were commanded to place it in the Holy of Holies beside the Ark of the Covenant.</p>\n<p>In continuation of this, every community is commanded to place its Torah scrolls in a splendid ark built in the most honoured place at the front of the synagogue; people strive to build it in the most beautiful and magnificent way, and out of respect they call it the 'Holy Ark' (Aron Ha-Kodesh) (Shabbat 32a; Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 282:1).</p>\n<p>The Sages said (Avot 4:6): \"Whoever desecrates the Torah – his person is desecrated before people, and whoever honours the Torah – his person is honoured before people\". A person's honour derives from the fact that there is a Divine spark in him, and the more he honours the Torah, the more that spark shines in him and he becomes honoured. On the other hand, the more he degrades the Torah, the more that spark is covered and disappears, and he remains empty, hollow and desecrated.</p>\n<p>The Sages told of Aḥav king of Israel, who was very wicked: when the army of Aram conquered the cities of Shomron and besieged his capital, Ben-Hadad king of Aram proposed that he surrender and give him all his silver and gold, his wives and his children. And Aḥav agreed, for his situation was desperate. But when he demanded that he also hand over the Torah scroll, he did not agree (I Melakhim 20:1-9). In the merit of this he was saved from Aram and defeated them, and merited to reign twenty-two years, corresponding to the twenty-two letters of the Torah (Sanhedrin 102b). On the other hand, regarding Yehoyakim king of Yehuda, who degraded the words of prophecy written on parchment and burned them in fire, it was decreed that \"his dead body shall be cast out in the day to the heat, and in the night to the frost\" (Yirmeyahu 36:9-30).</p>\n<p>Included in honouring the Torah scroll is that when it is being carried one must stand in its honour (below halakha 2), and it is forbidden to turn one's back to it (below halakha 3), and when it is carried one must carry it respectfully (below halakha 4). Likewise, a person must take care not to spit facing a Torah scroll, not to uncover his nakedness facing it, and not to stretch out his legs towards it in a disrespectful manner (Rambam; Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 282:1).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The Sages said (Avot 4:6): \"Whoever honours the Torah – his person is honoured before people, and whoever desecrates the Torah – his person is desecrated before people\". The commentators explained that included in honouring the Torah is the obligation to honour the Torah scroll (the commentary attributed to Rashi, Rabbeinu Yona, Magen Avot of the Rashbatz, the Bartenura and others). And so wrote the Rambam (Torah Scroll 10:11). The Mabit explained (Kiryat Sefer on the Rambam there) that this obligation is from the Torah. The Peri Megadim (introduction to Mishbetzot Zahav 153) explains that honouring a Torah scroll is from the Torah, as the Sages learned in Kiddushin (33b) that one must rise before it by a kal va-ḥomer from the honour of a Torah scholar, before whom one must rise by Torah law. But using the money of a Torah scroll or objects of holiness for mundane purposes is forbidden rabbinically. The Gedolei Ha-Kodesh (Yoreh De'ah 282:3) disagreed and held that even rising before a Torah scroll is rabbinic, since the kal va-ḥomer is an asmakhta, and only a very severe degradation, such as bringing it into a lavatory, is forbidden by Torah law. And likewise the Imrei Yosher (2:199) distinguished between a great degradation, which is forbidden by Torah law, and a small degradation, which is forbidden rabbinically. And it seems that honouring the Torah scroll is included in the mitzva of sanctifying the Name and the prohibition of desecrating it, as it is said (Vayikra 22:32): \"And you shall not profane My holy name, but I will be hallowed among the children of Israel; I am the Lord who hallows you\" (Rambam, Foundations of the Torah 5:1). It is also included in the mitzva of loving God and fearing Him, as it is said (Devarim 6:5): \"And you shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your might\", and it is said (there 6:13): \"You shall fear the Lord your God\" (Rambam there 2:1). However, the parameters of the mitzva were determined by the Sages. Similarly the Yere'im wrote (409) that the mitzva 'you shall revere My sanctuary' also includes an obligation to honour synagogues, and it was the Sages who defined the rules of honour in the Temple and the rules of honour in a synagogue (see below 12, note 1). Accordingly, it is reasonable that a flagrant affront to the honour of the Torah scroll, such as burning it or handing it over to enemies of Israel who will degrade it, is forbidden by Torah law. Whereas a slight affront, including not keeping the laws the Sages established to protect its honour, is forbidden rabbinically (and so emerges from the discussions of the poskim)."}]},{"slug":"21-09-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-02/","title_he":"ב – עמידה בפני ספר תורה","title_en":"2. Standing Before a Torah Scroll","body_en":"<p>When the Torah scroll is carried, and likewise when it is raised, everyone who sees it must rise until it is put down in its place in the ark or on the bima. It is a kal va-ḥomer: if before Torah scholars it is a mitzva by Torah law to stand in order to honour the Torah they have learned, as it is said (Vayikra 19:32): \"and you shall honour the face of the elder\", all the more so is it a mitzva to stand before the Torah scroll itself (Kiddushin 33b). Even one who is engaged in Torah study must rise before the Torah scroll. Even one who hears the sound of the bells on the Torah scroll and understands that it is being carried must stand (Shulḥan Arukh and Rema, Yoreh De'ah 282:2; Pitḥei Teshuva 3). And even on Shabbat, out of honour for the Torah, so that people will stand before it, it is permitted to carry the Torah scroll with bells that make a sound (Magen Avraham 338:1). It is customary to accompany the Torah scroll a little when it is carried from the Holy Ark to the bima and when it is returned (Rema, Oraḥ Ḥayim 149:1).</p>\n<p>The obligation to stand before the Torah scroll when it is carried applies on condition that the Torah scroll is in the same domain, for example when it is in the synagogue and the Torah scroll is carried from the ark to the bima. Likewise, one who is sitting outside and sees that the Torah scroll is being carried outside – must stand. But one who is sitting outside the synagogue, or in a room adjoining the synagogue, and sees through the window or the doorway that the Torah scroll is being carried, since he is in another domain, need not stand (Beit Yosef and Rema, Yoreh De'ah 282:2).</p>\n<p>After the Torah scroll has been put down in its place – in the Holy Ark, on the bima, or in the lap of a person sitting on a chair – one need not stand, because the scroll is in the place designated for it at that time (Beit Yosef and Rema 242:18; Mishna Berura 146:17).</p>\n<p>When the Holy Ark is opened during the recitation of special prayers, by the letter of the law there is no obligation to stand. First, because the Torah scroll is in its proper place. Second, it is in the ark, which is another domain. Nevertheless, people are accustomed to enhance the mitzva of honouring the Torah and to stand, since the ark is normally closed, and when it is open it is fitting to honour the Torah that is revealed to the public (Taz, Yoreh De'ah 242:13). But one who is weak and finds it hard to stand may sit, and only when the Torah scroll is carried from the heikhal to the bima and from the bima to the heikhal, and likewise during the raising of the Torah – should he make the effort to stand.</p>\n<p>When dancing with the Torah scrolls on Simḥat Torah, it is right for everyone present in the synagogue to take part in the dancing, or at least to stand around the dancers in the way people stand when the Torah scroll is carried. But one who finds it hard to stand and take part in the dancing may sit at the sides of the synagogue, for one can say that during the dancing the place of the Torah scroll is in the hands of the dancers, and it is as though the scroll were resting on the bima. Similarly, just as after the rav has reached his place in the synagogue, or the place from which he will give the class, one need not continue standing, even though the rav is standing and even walking back and forth – so there is no obligation to stand when dancing with the Torah (Peninei Halakha, Sukkot 7, note 3).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"21-09-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-03/","title_he":"ג – שלא להפנות את הגב לספר התורה","title_en":"3. Not Turning One's Back to the Torah Scroll","body_en":"<p>It is part of honouring the Torah scroll that a person should not turn his back to it, as the High Priest did when he left the Holy of Holies: he did not turn his back and leave, but left with his face towards the Holy. And likewise the priests and the Levites, when they finished their service in the Temple and went home, walked backwards with their faces towards the Holy. And a student leaving his principal teacher should not turn his back to him and go, but should leave with his face towards his teacher (Yoma 53a; Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 282:1). The face is the essence of a person, for in it are his seeing eyes, his speaking mouth, his smelling nose, and towards it his hearing ears are directed. Therefore, when a person turns his back to something, it is a sign that at that moment he is ignoring it. And even if before that he turned towards it, that was like the way a person uses his utensils as needed, and after finishing using them he turns his back to them. But the Torah scroll has an intrinsic and absolute value, and therefore even after finishing one's engagement with it, one should not turn one's back to it.</p>\n<p>If the Torah scroll was resting on a place higher than ten tefaḥim, and all the more so when it is inside an ark, since it is in another domain, it is permitted to turn one's back to it. Therefore it is permitted to turn one's back to the Holy Ark when walking towards the exit of the synagogue (Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 282:1; Taz 1). However, upon reaching the entrance of the synagogue it is good to turn back to face the Holy Ark, bow, and leave (Rema, Oraḥ Ḥayim 132:2; Magen Avraham there 6).</p>\n<p>Close to the Holy Ark, honour and affront are more conspicuous, and therefore it is customary not to turn one's back close to the Holy Ark even when it is closed. But when there is a need, it is permitted to stand with one's back to the Holy Ark, as the priests blessing the people or the rav giving a class stand with their faces to the congregation and their backs to the Holy Ark. In the Temple too, when the priests blessed the people with the Priestly Blessing, they stood with their faces towards the people and their backs to the heikhal, out of respect for the public (Sota 40a). It is a fine custom for rabbis to kiss the curtain of the Holy Ark before beginning to preach before the congregation; this is like taking permission to speak before the congregation, and asking forgiveness for having to turn their backs to the Holy Ark.</p>\n<p>Although it is permitted occasionally to turn one's back to the Holy Ark when it is closed, it is not right to do so on a fixed basis; therefore one should not set seats in the synagogue whose backs face the Holy Ark. The rabbis and elders too, who sit with their faces towards the congregation, sit at the sides of the Holy Ark, so that they do not turn their backs to the Holy Ark (below 13:7). The seats between the bima and the Holy Ark too are set facing the Holy Ark. And although during the Torah reading it turns out that the backs of those sitting there face the scroll, there is no prohibition in this, since at that time the scroll is resting in its place on the bima, which is higher than ten tefaḥim, and their backs face the scroll only for a short while (Rema, Oraḥ Ḥayim 150:5; Taz 2; Peri Megadim; Mishna Berura 14).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The Rambam wrote (Torah Scroll 10:10): \"And he should not turn his back to a Torah scroll, unless it was ten tefaḥim higher than him\". And so in the Shulḥan Arukh (Yoreh De'ah 282:1). All the more so when the scroll rests in the Holy Ark, for in addition to the fact that it is generally above a height of ten tefaḥim, the ark, whose size is at least an amma by an amma by three ammot, is a domain of its own (Peri Megadim, Oraḥ Ḥayim 141, Mishbetzot Zahav 3, at the end). And especially when it is closed.<br>However, beyond the precise law regarding the Torah scroll, people treat the Holy Ark too with honour. And therefore even when it is closed, those standing near it take care not to turn their backs to it, and only when necessary, during the Priestly Blessing and the giving of a class or a sermon, out of respect for the public, do they face the congregation and turn their backs to the ark. Likewise, out of honour for the Holy Ark it is customary not to set in the synagogue fixed chairs whose backs face the Holy Ark, but for the purpose of study in ḥavruta or in a group, it is permitted. And close to the Holy Ark, without a special need, it is right to be stringent; and so wrote Rabbi Yesha'ya Pik (Minei Targuma, Likkutim). And it is possible that beyond four ammot there is no need for concern (see Ḥavot Ya'ir 184).<br>During the reading of the haftara, according to the custom of those of Ashkenazi origin, the scroll rests in a person's lap, and likewise during the Torah reading when two Torah scrolls are taken out; and since it is then in its place, one need not stand before it. The Peri Megadim (141, Mishbetzot Zahav 3) and the Mishna Berura (147:29) wrote that the one holding the scroll should take care to move a little to the side so that the reader and the one called up to the Torah do not turn their backs to it. However, many are not concerned about this, since it is occasional, and the one called up and the reader are standing in their natural place and are engaged in a mitzva (Levushei Mordekhai, Oraḥ Ḥayim, third edition 15; Halikhot Shlomo, Prayer 12:14). It seems that those sitting in front of the Torah scroll should turn themselves to the side, so that they will not have their backs to the Torah scroll that is in his lap, since it is not in another domain."}]},{"slug":"21-09-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-04/","title_he":"ד – הולכת ספר תורה","title_en":"4. Carrying a Torah Scroll","body_en":"<p>Sometimes it is necessary to move the Torah scroll from place to place, and then the one carrying the Torah scroll must carry it upright in his arms against his heart, respectfully. He should not place it as a load on his head or on his shoulder, or in a bag behind his back (Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 282:3). When the Torah scroll is transported in a car, one should try to have another passenger join the driver who will carry the Torah scroll in his arms, and there will be no one who turns his back to it. If there is no other passenger there, the Torah scroll should be placed in a respectful and stable way on the seats of the car. When necessary, it may be placed in a suitcase, or wrapped in a tallit, in the boot (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan, Yoreh De'ah 282:6).<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>Out of honour for the Torah scroll, one should go to it, to the synagogue, in order to read from it, and not bring it to a private house and read from it there. Even for one who through circumstances beyond his control cannot come to the synagogue, such as a sick person or a prisoner, the Torah scroll is not brought. But if a proper and respectful place is prepared for the Torah scroll, where it will rest for a day or two, it is permitted to bring it for the need of an individual in order to read from it with a minyan, for in this way it does not appear that the scroll is being moved about for the sake of a person. And likewise many are accustomed to bring a Torah scroll to a house of mourners for a number of days (Shulḥan Arukh and Rema, Oraḥ Ḥayim 135:14).</p>\n<p>All this is because it is not in keeping with the honour of the Torah scroll to bring it for an individual, but it is permitted to bring it for a person great in Torah who through circumstances beyond his control cannot come to the synagogue (Yerushalmi Yoma 7:1; Beit Yosef and Rema there). It is likewise permitted to bring it for a minyan that cannot pray in the synagogue (Bei'ur Halakha there, s.v. 'Ein'). Therefore, it is permitted to bring a Torah scroll to a minyan of soldiers training in the field. And it is likewise permitted to carry the Torah scroll from the synagogue to the minyan of a family holding a celebration.<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>It is permitted to take the Torah scroll out when taking it out is in honour of the Torah, for example to dance with the Torah scrolls in the street of the city on Simḥat Torah (Yabi'a Omer 7, Oraḥ Ḥayim 56), and likewise at the dedication of a Torah scroll (Ikkarei Ha-Dat 8:28), as will be explained in the next halakha.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"When there is a need to send the Torah scroll in a parcel by plane or by ship, some are accustomed to open the stitching between two of the sheets, and thereby its validity is temporarily cancelled, and there will not be so great an affront to its honour in the jolting of the journey (Kaf Ha-Ḥayim, Oraḥ Ḥayim 135:74). On the other hand, some say that one should not undo the sheets, because thereby one lowers the Torah scroll from its sanctity and affronts its honour; rather, one should place it in a suitcase in a respectful and protected manner (Imrei Yosher, part 2, 171:2)."},{"n":4,"en":"I have written according to the middle path agreed upon by most poskim. However, some are lenient to bring a Torah scroll even for an individual who through circumstances beyond his control cannot come to the synagogue (the Or Zarua, brought in the Darkhei Moshe), and for the reading of parashat 'Zakhor', which is from the Torah, one should be lenient (Magen Avraham 135:23; Mishna Berura 46). On the other hand, some are stringent not to bring a Torah scroll at all for an individual, such as for prayer in a house of mourning, even when it is brought for several days (as brought in the Kaf Ha-Ḥayim 135:75), and likewise not to bring it for a great person or for the reading of parashat 'Zakhor' (as brought there 135:84-85)."}]},{"slug":"21-09-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-05/","title_he":"ה – חגיגת הכנסת ספר תורה","title_en":"5. The Celebration of Dedicating a Torah Scroll","body_en":"<p>Israel are accustomed to hold a great celebration when they finish writing a Torah scroll, and they carry the Torah scroll in procession and with dancing to the synagogue. As a kind of source for this we learn from the way the Ark of God was brought up to Jerusalem, as told in the book of Shmuel (II 6:5): \"And David and all the house of Israel played before the Lord with all manner of instruments made of cypress wood, and with lyres, and with harps, and with timbrels, and with sistra, and with cymbals\". But Uzza was not careful about the honour of the Ark and died (there 6:6-7), and the celebration was cut short, and the Ark remained in the house of Oved-Edom the Gittite, who was a Levite. After three months, when it was told to David that God had blessed the house of Oved-Edom and all that he had, David took courage to bring the Ark up to Jerusalem with a great celebration and the offering of sacrifices. And they made great rejoicing there, as it is said (there 6:14-19): \"And David danced before the Lord with all his might… So David and all the house of Israel brought up the Ark of the Lord with shouting and with the sound of the shofar… And he distributed to all the people, to the whole multitude of Israel, both men and women – to each one a loaf of bread, and a good portion (a portion of the meat of the bulls they had offered), and a flagon (of drink)\". And so did his son Shlomo when he brought the Ark into the Temple after he had finished building it (I Melakhim 8; Shabbat 30a).</p>\n<p>Similarly, it is customary to bring the Torah scroll into the synagogue with song and dancing, and to distribute to the children kinds of sweets and illuminated decorations, like the torches that were at the gathering at Mount Sinai.</p>\n<p>The procession begins at the house of the donor of the scroll or of the rav, where it is customary to finish writing the last letters of the Torah. When the Torah scroll is carried, a canopy is spread over it in its honour, and rabbis and dignitaries are honoured with carrying it. When the procession approaches the synagogue, it is customary to take out the Torah scrolls that are in the ark to meet the new scroll and to dance with them (Birkei Yosef, Oraḥ Ḥayim 135:13). Many are accustomed to say then the verses of accepting the yoke of the kingdom of Heaven.</p>\n<p>After entering the synagogue, the Torah scroll is placed on the bima, and it is right that the donor of the scroll recite 'She-heḥeyanu' (see Peninei Halakha, Berakhot 17:9). And if the congregation initiated its writing in partnership, one of them should recite 'She-heḥeyanu' on behalf of all (Yehuda Ya'aleh, Yoreh De'ah 282). While the scroll is on the bima, some are accustomed to read from it verses from the end of the Torah without a blessing, and the Torah is raised with great honour. After that the scroll is brought into the ark with song and with the recitation of the verses customarily said every Shabbat when it is returned. And it is good to give then a sermon on the value of the Torah and its study (Me'am Lo'ez, Devarim 860). If Minḥa is prayed then, Taḥanun is not said in it.</p>\n<p>After that a respectable se'udat mitzva is held for the completion of the Torah, and words of Torah are said at it. It is right to bring several wines to the meal, and the donor of the scroll should recite 'Ha-tov ve-ha-metiv' over the second wine (see Peninei Halakha, Berakhot 17:9).<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"There are two aspects to the rejoicing: a) over bringing the scroll into the synagogue, b) over completing the great mitzva of writing a Torah scroll, for at every completion of a mitzva there is rejoicing (Bava Batra 131b; Magen Avraham 551:33; Da'at Torah 669:1; Mishneh Sakhir, Oraḥ Ḥayim 215). All the more so with so great a mitzva, which is like a continuation of the giving of the Torah (see Menaḥot 30a; Beit Yosef 270:1). Therefore even one who writes a scroll for himself – it is a mitzva for him to make a celebration.<br>In our days many are accustomed to finish the writing close to the beginning of the celebration, and they honour distinguished people with writing the letters, each one a letter. But since the great majority of people do not know how to write with a quill properly, some are accustomed that the scribe outlines the frame of the letters and the people fill in their interior (Sho'el U-Meshiv, first edition 3:102). In practice, since the excitement is great, even in this people sometimes fail, and the ink runs, and by the time they correct the scroll a very long time will pass. Therefore, it is customary that the scribe is the one who fills in the letters, and the dignitaries place their hand on him as a kind of partners."}]},{"slug":"21-09-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-06/","title_he":"ו – כבוד ספר תורה, נ\"ך ודברי חכמים","title_en":"6. Honouring the Torah Scroll, the Nakh and the Words of the Sages","body_en":"<p>Because of the sanctity of the Torah scroll, one should not touch the parchment of the scroll with bare hands. And when there is a need to adjust the parchment while rolling the scroll, one wraps one's hand in a cloth or a tallit and adjusts it in that way. However, for a great need, such as for repairing the scroll, it is permitted to touch the parchment with bare hands, and it is good to wash one's hands beforehand (Megilla 32b; Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 147:1; Mishna Berura 1-2).</p>\n<p>It is permitted to touch the rollers of the scroll, and that is how the Torah scroll is raised. It is likewise permitted to touch the mantle or the case in which the scroll is kept, and that is how it is carried. Women too, during their period of nidda, are permitted to carry the Torah scroll, kiss it and read from it (Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 282:9). It is permitted to touch with bare hands the parchment of books of the Prophets and of scrolls written in ink, and it is good to wash one's hands beforehand (Rema 147:1).<sup>6</sup></p>\n<p>The sanctity of the Torah is greater than the sanctity of the 'Prophets' and the 'Writings', and therefore it is forbidden to place a book of the Nakh on top of a book of the Torah. But it is permitted to place one book of the Ḥumash on top of another. When the Torah, the Prophets and the Writings are bound together (a Tanakh), it is permitted to read in the Writings, even though at that time the pages of the Prophets and the Writings are lying on top of the pages of the Torah (Shulḥan Arukh and Rema 282:19).</p>\n<p>Although the level of prophecy with which the Prophets were written is higher than the level of the holy spirit with which the Writings were written, the Prophets and the Writings are considered to be at the same level of sanctity, and therefore it is permitted to place them on top of one another (the Ran, Megilla 8b; the Ḥatam Sofer on the Shulḥan Arukh 153:2; Orot Ha-Kodesh 1, p. 270; Peninei Halakha, Emuna U-Mitzvoteha 17:1).</p>\n<p>One should not place books of the Oral Torah, such as the Mishna, on top of the Nakh (Rabbi Yosef ibn Migash 92). Among books of the Oral Torah there is no obligation to be particular, but they are all equal, and even books of the Aḥaronim may be placed on books of the Talmud.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"Megilla (32a): \"Rabbi Parnakh said in the name of Rabbi Yoḥanan: Whoever holds a Torah scroll naked – is buried naked. Can you think 'naked'?! Rather say: he is buried naked without mitzvot. Can you think 'without mitzvot'?! Rather Abaye said: he is buried naked without that mitzva.\" The Rishonim explained that the prohibition is to touch the Torah scroll with bare hands (Rashi and the Rivevan there; Rabbeinu Yehonatan of Lunel and the Ran on the Rif there). Some add that one who touches the Torah scroll while studying it loses the mitzva he fulfilled in his study (Tosafot there, s.v. 'Be-lo ota'; the Rosh, Megilla 4:12; the Riaz, the Ra'avan). Even if he washes his hands first, it is forbidden to touch the Torah scroll with bare hands (the Ramban, the Rashba and the Me'iri, Shabbat 14a; Tosafot and the Agudda, Shabbat 18a). And there is one who holds that after washing hands it is permitted to touch the Torah scroll (the Ra'avya, brought in the Mordekhai). Simply, the prohibition on touching applies only to a Torah scroll, but some are stringent also with books of the Nakh written in ink on parchment (Tosafot, Shabbat 14a, s.v. 'Ha-oḥez'; the Agudda there). In practice people are accustomed to be lenient, and the Rema wrote (147:1) that it is good to wash one's hands beforehand. However, the immigrants from Yemen, when the scroll must be rolled and it is a great bother to do so with the help of a cloth, are accustomed to roll it while touching the parchment, for they learned in the Rambam (STaM 10:6) that the prohibition is that a person who is not clothed, even if his nakedness is covered, should touch the Torah scroll (Rav Kafiḥ, Ḥevel Naḥalato 22:4). But as a rule they were careful about this, as they are accustomed during the raising of the Torah to raise the parchment with the help of a cloth (below 15:14; Divrei Ḥakhamim 49, by Rav Shalom Yitzḥak Ha-Levi)."},{"n":7,"en":"Some take extra care not to place the Writings on the Prophets (based on Rabbeinu Baḥya, Vayikra 8:8), and not to place books of the Aḥaronim on those of the Rishonim, nor the Rishonim on the Mishna and the Talmud.<br>In the days of the Sages there were those who were accustomed to store the teruma they had separated for the priests near the sacred books in order to keep it in purity, and sometimes mice would come to eat the teruma and in the process would also eat the sacred books. To prevent this they enacted that the sacred books have the status of 'second degree of impurity', and consequently teruma that touches them becomes invalid, and likewise one who touched them with his hands – his hands became impure, and in order to purify them he had to wash his hands (Mishna Yadayim 2:2-5; Shabbat 14a). Today, after the purification with the ashes of the red heifer has ceased, everyone is impure, and this enactment is not observed (Bei'ur Halakha 164:2, s.v. 'Laḥzor')."}]},{"slug":"21-09-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-07/","title_he":"ז – ספר שנשרף או נפל","title_en":"7. A Scroll That Was Burned or Fell","body_en":"<p>One who sees a Torah scroll or passages of the sacred writings being burned or destroyed high-handedly, out of spite, must make two tears in his garment, one for the sanctified parchment and one for the letters (Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 340:37). This tearing expresses the pain and the shock at the affront to the honour of Heaven, and it contains a certain repair of the desecration of God's name caused by the wicked. But if the sacred writings were burned or lost unintentionally, even when it is a fire that consumed many books, one does not tear (Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 340:37; Ḥakham Tzvi 17).</p>\n<p>The Sages learned this halakha from what is told in the Prophets about King Yehoyakim: after the words of Yirmeyahu's prophecy about the destruction were read before him, he cut the scroll with a razor and threw it into the fire. As it is said (Yirmeyahu 36:23-24): \"And it came to pass, when Yehudi had read three or four columns (verses), that he cut it with the scribe's knife and cast it into the fire that was in the brazier, until all the scroll was consumed in the fire that was in the brazier. Yet they were not afraid, nor did they tear their garments, neither the king nor any of his servants who heard all these words.\" Thus those who witness the degradation of the sacred writings must tear their garments (Mo'ed Katan 26a). And who knows, perhaps if they had torn their garments out of shock at the degradation of the word of God, they would have been roused to repentance, and would not have been punished with destruction and exile.<sup>8</sup></p>\n<p>In continuation of this, Israel were accustomed that if a person mistakenly dropped a Torah scroll or tefillin on the ground, he should fast for one day. And although there is no halakhic obligation in this, because of the severity of the degradation of the sacred writings caused by him, people were accustomed to be roused to repentance through a fast. For a Torah scroll people were accustomed to fast even if it fell with its cover, and for tefillin only if they fell without their case, and if they fell in their case – one gives a little charity (Maharai Bruna 127; Mishpetei Shmuel 12; Mishna Berura 40:3; above 3:17).</p>\n<p>Some were stringent from the outset also regarding those who see the Torah scroll fall – that they should fast for one day, for the fact that they witnessed the degradation of the holy is a sign that they must be roused to repentance, and perhaps if they had been more watchful, the scroll would not have fallen. In addition, people were accustomed to rouse the members of the community in which this happened to repentance in matters requiring strengthening, each rav, according to his knowledge of his community, instructing them to strengthen what needed repair in it. And some were stringent with all who saw it that they should fast three days, and with the rest of the members of the community that they should fast one day. However, since there is no obligation in this, one for whom the fast was difficult was permitted to redeem it with charity (Responsa Ḥayim Sha'al 1:12; Divrei Ḥayim 1, Yoreh De'ah 59).</p>\n<p>Today, it is customary to rouse the public to repentance through strengthening in Torah study and in guarding its honour, and in other mitzvot as needed; and as for the fasts, it is customary for each person to redeem them for the price he is prepared to pay so as not to have to fast one day. And one for whom fasts are easy – should fast. And it is customary that one from whose hand the Torah scroll fell should fast even when the fast is hard for him, and only if the fast is very hard for him should he redeem it with a large amount of charity.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"From here we learn that also for the deliberate destruction of passages of the Nakh one must tear one's garment, as for the burning of the Torah (the Baḥ 340:35; Taz 24; Shakh 56; Misgeret Ha-Shulḥan on Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 340:37; Yad Sha'ul 36; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 38; Ḥazon Ovadia, Mourning 1, p. 260). And some say that one need not tear (Shevet Yehuda of Rabbi Yehuda Ayash 340:66). Also for a burning by a gentile one tears (the Baḥ; Taz, Yoreh De'ah 340:24; Shakh 56; Misgeret Ha-Shulḥan). And some say that one tears only for a burning by a Jew (Ḥakham Tzvi 17). Some say that also on seeing the deliberate burning of books of the Oral Torah one is obligated to tear (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 340:38), and some were in doubt about this (Tiferet La-Moshe, Pitḥei Teshuva 340:21). About 800 years ago (5002, 1242), a disaster befell the Jewish people when the wicked kingdom of France decreed to burn in Paris twenty-four wagonloads of precious handwritten volumes of the Talmud. Following this, individuals were accustomed for hundreds of years to fast on the Friday before parashat Ḥukkat (Mishna Berura 580:16)."},{"n":9,"en":"If the one who dropped the Torah scroll had not eaten or drunk until that time, it is preferable that he continue fasting that whole day, and say 'Anenu' at Minḥa. And if he had already eaten or drunk, he should take upon himself to fast on another day (Maharai Bruna 127). And if it is very hard for him to fast, he should redeem it with a large amount of charity (see Shulḥan Gavoha 580:4, and Mayim Ḥayim of Rabbi Mashash, Oraḥ Ḥayim 1:305)."}]},{"slug":"21-09-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-08/","title_he":"ח – איסור מכירת ספר תורה ודין ספרים מודפסים","title_en":"8. The Prohibition on Selling a Torah Scroll, and the Law of Printed Books","body_en":"<p>Because of the great level of sanctity of a Torah scroll, the Sages said (Megilla 27a) that one should not sell a Torah scroll. Even one who has several Torah scrolls should not sell one of them. And if he sold it, he will never see a sign of blessing from it. Even when the owner of the Torah scroll has nothing to eat except with difficulty, or has no money to buy items needed for a mitzva, such as tefillin and a mezuza, he should not sell his Torah scroll. Only for the sake of fulfilling two important mitzvot, Torah study and marriage, is he permitted to sell a Torah scroll, if he has no other way to finance their fulfilment (Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 270:1). This law applies also to a congregation, which may not sell a Torah scroll in its possession unless it needs to sell it in order to sustain Torah study or to marry off the poor (Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 153:6). The prohibition is on selling a Torah scroll, but it is permitted to give it as a gift to a congregation that needs it (Mishna Berura 153:68).<sup>10</sup></p>\n<p>Just as it is forbidden to sell a Torah scroll, so it is forbidden to sell printed books intended for study, when selling them is liable to cause neglect of Torah study (see above 8:2). But if selling them will not cause neglect of Torah study, because in any case no one studies in them, or for example because books in a new and more beautiful edition were bought in their place, it is permitted to sell them. And one who estimates that he will not find buyers for the books of the old editions would do well to announce in his surroundings that anyone interested in them can receive them for free, so as not to put into geniza books in which people might still study. And if no one is found who wants the books, it is permitted to put them in geniza. And one should not refrain from buying books in new editions in order to avoid putting the old ones in geniza, for the mitzva of Torah study is the main thing, and one may hope that through buying the new and beautiful books he will study more Torah.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"Megilla (27a): \"The Rabbis taught: A person should not sell a Torah scroll even though he does not need it. Furthermore, Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel said: Even if he has nothing to eat and he sold a Torah scroll… he will never see a sign of blessing.\" And so is the law of a congregation, as explained in the Shulḥan Arukh (Oraḥ Ḥayim 153:6). And so wrote the Rambam (Torah Scroll 10:2) and the Me'iri. And so wrote Rabbeinu Avraham min Ha-Har and the Rivash (285), and they added that even the seven leaders of the town are forbidden to sell it. On the other hand, some wrote that in a pressing situation the seven leaders of the town, and even an individual, are permitted to sell the Torah scroll for other purposes too, except that one does not see a sign of blessing from it (the Ra'avya, Megilla 590; the Mordekhai 824; the Agudda; the Shiltei Ha-Gibborim). And some permitted the seven leaders of the town to sell it without mentioning that one does not see a sign of blessing (the Tur; 'some say' in the Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 153:10). Apparently, in the hard days of the exile, when Israel were in great distress, people were accustomed in a pressing situation to be lenient and sell a Torah scroll also for purposes other than marriage and Torah study, and since this was the practice, it was on that understanding that they bought or wrote the Torah scrolls (Magen Avraham 153:22). And it seems that even those who are lenient would agree that the seller does not see a sign of blessing, as brought in the Gemara, and therefore they were lenient only in a pressing situation (Olat Tamid 153:30; Eliya Rabba 22; Mishna Berura 60). And it seems that today, when we are not in distress, there is no room to be lenient for the seven leaders of the town or for an individual to sell a Torah scroll for other purposes, for today people do not buy a Torah scroll on the understanding that this leniency will be applied, and the law has returned to its basic form, that only for marriage and Torah study is it permitted in a pressing situation to sell a Torah scroll.<br>See Peninei Halakha, Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 5:21, note 21, that some say the permission to sell a Torah scroll applies only to one who has not fulfilled the mitzva of being fruitful and multiplying (Shulḥan Arukh, Even Ha-Ezer 1:8), and most Rishonim hold that it applies also to one who has fulfilled the mitzva of being fruitful and multiplying, so that he may merit more children (the Behag, the She'iltot, the Rosh, Beit Shmuel 1:16). And some permit it also for marriage to a woman who cannot give birth (the Ramban, the Maharshal, Ḥelkat Meḥokek 1:10).<br>If the Torah scroll became invalid, it is permitted to sell it like single books of the Ḥumash, and one must buy a Torah scroll with its proceeds. And if it was sold by the decision of the seven leaders of the town – it is permitted to buy other public needs with its proceeds (the Rivash 285; Beit Yosef 153:3; Rema 282:18)."},{"n":11,"en":"The Rosh wrote (Torah Scroll 1) that just as it is forbidden to sell a Torah scroll, so one should not sell the books in which he studies \"except in order to study Torah and to marry a wife\". And so wrote the Shulḥan Arukh (270:2): \"Nowadays it is a mitzva to write books of the Ḥumash of the Torah, the Mishna, the Gemara and their commentaries, and he should not sell them except in order to study Torah and to marry a wife\". But there is a difficulty, for there is a law that if a person sold all his property – his books too are included, and only regarding a Torah scroll is there a doubt whether it is included in this (Bava Batra 150b; Beit Yosef and Shulḥan Arukh, Ḥoshen Mishpat 248:11); thus the law of sacred books is not like the law of a Torah scroll. And so wrote in halakha the Leḥem Ḥamudot, the Eliya Rabba (Oraḥ Ḥayim 153:22), the Magen Gibborim (26), the Shulḥan Gavoha (Yoreh De'ah 270:13) and the Kol Ya'akov (8). And in the Tiferet La-Moshe (siman 270, brought in the Pitḥei Teshuva there 11) he explained that it is permitted to sell sacred books because it was on that understanding that they were acquired. But the difficulty remains, for in the Shulḥan Arukh (Yoreh De'ah 270:2) it is ruled that one should not sell sacred books. Many explained that he means that it is not fitting to sell them; and so wrote the Shiyurei Knesset Ha-Gedola (270, Hagahot Beit Yosef 9), the Birkei Yosef (270:10), the Yafeh La-Lev (17) and the Sho'el Ve-Nish'al (5, Yoreh De'ah 123). However, it seems that all would agree that if as a result of selling the books he will be kept from studying, it is forbidden to sell, and in such a case there is a prohibition, and so I wrote above. And this seems to be the intention of the Rosh and the Shulḥan Arukh (270:2), who forbade selling books if selling them will cause neglect of Torah study (and see Igrot Moshe, Oraḥ Ḥayim 4:72). But there is no prohibition on selling books when he has books with which he can fulfil the mitzva of Torah study."}]},{"slug":"21-09-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-09/","title_he":"ט – כבוד ספרי קודש מודפסים","title_en":"9. Honouring Printed Sacred Books","body_en":"<p>After it was permitted to write the Oral Torah, it is a mitzva to treat the books of the Oral Torah with honour, and it is likewise a mitzva to treat printed sacred books with honour, such as Ḥumashim and siddurim, volumes of the Gemara and books of the Rishonim and the Aḥaronim (below 11:15, note 18). And although their sanctity is not like the sanctity of a Torah scroll written in ink on parchment, they have sanctity and must be treated with honour (Taz, Yoreh De'ah 271:8). Included in the mitzva is preparing for them a fine bookcase and shelves, arranging them there respectfully, and returning them to their place at the end of study. The Pele Yo'etz wrote (entry 'Sefer'): \"A person is obligated to keep his books pleasant and to shake the dust off them from time to time, and to treat them with great honour, to hold them respectfully and to set them well in their place, and not to behave before them in a disrespectful manner, for whoever degrades them – has despised the word of God, Heaven forbid, for they are considered the garments of the King\".<sup>12</sup></p>\n<p>One who wants to put a book down from his hand or pass it to his friend should not throw the book, but should put it down or give it respectfully (Eruvin 98a; Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 282:5). One should not leave a sacred book upside down, and one who sees a book upside down – should turn it over so as to set it upright, and it is a pious custom to kiss it when turning it over (Eruvin there; Darkhei Moshe and Rema there). One who wants to strike with his hand, the way people strike the table to make quiet or to emphasise something, should not bang on the book (Sefer Ḥasidim 923; Beit Leḥem Yehuda on the Shulḥan Arukh there).</p>\n<p>One should not place sacred books on the floor, but should place them in a respectable place. Merchants who need to store books, and are unable to place them on a place raised above the floor, should put something down, at least paper, to separate between the books and the floor (see Rema 282:7). However, it seems that when moving house or reorganising the shelves of the library, or when transporting books from the printer, when necessary it is permitted to place them on the ground, since everyone understands that it is for a short while.</p>\n<p>If a sacred book fell to the floor, one should pick it up quickly, and it is a pious custom to kiss it. But if many books fell, he should hurry to pick them up without stopping to kiss them, and should kiss only the last ones (see Pitḥei Teshuva 282:7).</p>\n<p>It is forbidden to sit on a bench or a bed on which a Torah scroll written in ink on parchment is lying, unless he places the Torah scroll on a place raised three tefaḥim above the bed or the bench, and in a pressing situation one may make do with raising it one tefaḥ (Mo'ed Katan 25a; Yerushalmi 3:5; Shakh 282:8).</p>\n<p>Likewise, it is right not to sit on a bed or a bench on which printed sacred books are lying, but he should raise them one tefaḥ. And if the place is crowded and he needs to sit there, he should place the sacred books on some raised surface, or upright (see Rema, Yoreh De'ah 282:7; Shakh 9; Radbaz 3:515).<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"Although the sanctity of sacred books is not like the sanctity of a Torah scroll written for the sake of sanctity in ink on parchment, one must treat them with honour, and it is forbidden to degrade them or to destroy them with one's own hands (Rambam's Commentary on the Mishna, Avot 4:6; Ḥavot Ya'ir 109; Maharashdam 184; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 282:11. On the laws of putting sacred books in geniza see below 11:16). Further on the honour of books, the Rambam wrote (Commentary on the Mishna, Avot 4:6): \"The honour of the Torah is that one honour its commandments by showing alacrity in performing them, and honour the Sages who carry it, and the books that were composed on it. And likewise the desecration of the Torah is in these three things\". And in Sefer Ḥasidim (275): \"Honour the Lord with your wealth (Mishlei 3:9) – honour Him more than your own wealth… so that if you see fire falling on your house – save from your house first the things of Heaven, such as books\". Since it was permitted to write the Oral Torah, books of the Oral Torah are defined as 'holiness' (Masat Binyamin 99; Peri Megadim 153, Mishbetzot Zahav 15; see below 14:4, 8), and some hold that their law is that of 'objects serving holiness', since their sanctity derives from the fact that they are used for the purpose of Torah study (Avnei Nezer, Yoreh De'ah 376)."},{"n":13,"en":"In Menaḥot (32b) it is brought that Rav Ḥelbo said in the name of Rav Huna that one should not sit on a bed on which a Torah scroll is lying, unlike the view of Rabba bar bar Ḥana in the name of Rabbi Yoḥanan, who permitted sitting on a bed on which a Torah scroll is lying. Some ruled like Rabbi Yoḥanan (Tosafot, Menaḥot there, s.v. 'De-amar'; the Mordekhai). But most Rishonim ruled like Rav Huna (Tosafot, Mo'ed Katan 25a, s.v. 'Ve-khaf'; the Rambam and the Ra'avad, Torah Scroll 10:6; the Ra'avya, the Semak, Beit Yosef 282:7). In the Yerushalmi (Berakhot 3:5) it is brought that if the scroll is raised it is permitted to sit on the bed; some hold that one must raise the scroll a tefaḥ and some say by any amount. Thus far. The Beit Yosef (282:7) wrote, in the name of the Ra'avad and Rabbeinu Manoaḥ (based on the Yerushalmi there), that one should raise the scroll a tefaḥ, and it is good to be stringent and raise it ten tefaḥim, and at least three tefaḥim. But the Shakh (282:8) wrote that by the letter of the law a tefaḥ is enough.<br>The Piskei Tosafot (Menaḥot 81) wrote that with books of the Talmud one may be lenient according to all views, for even with a Torah scroll Rabbi Yoḥanan is lenient. And so wrote Rabbeinu Manoaḥ, except that he was stringent to raise them by any amount, and at a time of crowding in the beit midrash one may be lenient. And in the Orḥot Ḥayim he wrote that one should not be lenient, so that people will not come to treat the books lightly. Their words were brought in the Beit Yosef and the Shakh 9. In practice, from the outset it is good to be stringent and raise them a tefaḥ, and in a pressing situation one may be lenient without raising them (Radbaz 3:515; Shiyurei Knesset Ha-Gedola 282:7; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 282:12, and others). And it is good to place them upright, so as to honour them as far as possible. And if it is a bench whose seats are separate, and the books are on an adjacent seat, one may be lenient (see Teshurat Shai 2:169; Shevet Ha-Levi 3:11; Az Nidberu 11:4:2). The Rema wrote (282:7) that from all that we have learned it follows all the more so that it is forbidden to place sacred books on the ground.<br>One should not sit or lean on a box or a bag in which there is a Torah scroll, and likewise one should not sit on a bag or a sack in which there are sacred books. But on a rigid box, if it is not designated specifically for sacred books and the weight of the one sitting does not press on the books inside it – it is permitted. And it is likewise permitted to sit on benches that have a storage compartment for books in their seat (Responsa of the Rema 34; Nekudot Ha-Kesef 282:1; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 282:12)."}]},{"slug":"21-09-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-10/","title_he":"י – כבוד ספרי קודש בעת הלימוד","title_en":"10. Honouring Sacred Books During Study","body_en":"<p>When a person needs to go out in the middle of his study, he should close the book and go out, for it is not in keeping with the book's honour that it remain open without anyone studying in it. And they said, by way of a segula, that one who leaves his book open – forgets his learning (Eruvin 98a; Taz and Shakh, Yoreh De'ah 277:1). But one who goes out for a short time need not close the book (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 277:2). It seems that a Torah scholar who is engaged in clarifying a sugya over several days, and makes use of many books, may leave them open, to make his study easier, so that he will not have to open them again and again.</p>\n<p>One should not use a sacred book other than for the purpose of study, for example to keep in it mundane things such as money, a document or a handkerchief, or to use it as a fan to blow air on one's face, or to stand it up in order to hide one's actions from one's friend or to make shade from the sun. However, for the purpose of study, when the sun dazzles the page one is studying and he very much needs it, it is permitted to use another book to make shade (Sefer Ḥasidim 903-904; Mishna Berura 154:31). And likewise, when necessary, it is permitted to place the sacred book in which he is studying on another sacred book in order to make his study easier (Mishna Berura there).<sup>14</sup></p>\n<p>Also during study one should not place a book of the 'Oral Torah' on a book of the 'Written Torah', or a book of the Nakh on the Torah (above halakha 6).</p>\n<p>One who finishes studying in a book he took from the library of the synagogue or the beit midrash should return it to its place, so that those who wish to study in it can find it, and in order to honour the book and the synagogue.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"According to the Taz (Yoreh De'ah 282:13), it is permitted to use a book in order to raise another book on condition that it was there from the start, or that both are books he needs for his study, but one should not bring a book specially in order to use it only for raising. But according to most Aḥaronim it is permitted to do so (Magen Avraham 154:14; Ḥayei Adam 31:48; Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh 28:9; Mishna Berura 154:31; Ḥazon Ovadia, Shabbat 5, p. 301). Similarly we find in Sefer Ḥasidim (903) that he forbade placing a book as a bookmark, and there (904) he permitted using it to shield from the sun while he is studying; thus for the purpose of present study he was lenient."}]},{"slug":"21-09-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-11/","title_he":"יא – כניסה לשירותים עם כתבי קודש","title_en":"11. Entering the Lavatory with Sacred Writings","body_en":"<p>It is forbidden to enter a lavatory (toilet) or a bathhouse with a Torah scroll, even if it is wrapped in several covers and placed inside its case (Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 282:4). Even the king of Israel, whose Torah scroll it is a mitzva to keep close to him wherever he goes, as it is said (Devarim 17:19): \"And it shall be with him, and he shall read in it all the days of his life\" – was forbidden to enter a lavatory or a bathhouse with the Torah scroll (Sanhedrin 21b).</p>\n<p>Likewise, it is forbidden to enter the lavatory with other sacred books, such as a siddur or a Ḥumash. But unlike a Torah scroll, if they are covered – it is permitted. Therefore, one who has a siddur or a Ḥumash in his pocket, if the pocket covers it completely, may enter the lavatory with it. And likewise when the Ḥumash or the siddur is tucked away inside a bag or a carrier bag, it is permitted to enter the lavatory with them. Likewise, one who has words of Torah written on a sheet of paper in his pocket is permitted to enter the lavatory, on condition that they are covered.<sup>15</sup></p>\n<p>It is permitted to enter the lavatory with a pendant in which the whole Tanakh is stored in minute writing, even if it is not covered, since a person cannot read it without the help of sophisticated decoding instruments. However, since a person knows that it contains the Tanakh, one should not degrade it (see Piskei Teshuva 22).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":15,"en":"In Berakhot (23a-b) it is told that when the Amora'im needed to enter the lavatory they would deposit the 'book of aggada' in their hands outside the lavatory. But when the book is covered it is agreed that it may be brought into the lavatory. And they disagreed about how many covers are required: some require two covers for sacred writings that contain holy names (Tashbetz Katan 420; Malbushei Yom Tov of Lipman 43:1; Sha'arei Teshuva 11; and similarly in Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 43:6; and Arukh Ha-Shulḥan, Yoreh De'ah 282:10). They learned this from the law of marital relations in a room in which there are tefillin or sacred books, which must be covered in a bag within a bag (see Peninei Halakha, Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 2:17). According to their view, placing them in a pocket is not enough, but if he wraps them in a carrier bag and puts them in his pocket, or they are in a pocket over which there is a sweater, or in an inside pocket that is under an additional layer of a coat or trousers – it is permitted. But according to most poskim it is enough to cover them with one cover, for we have learned (Shabbat 62a; Shulḥan Arukh 282:6) that it is permitted to enter the lavatory with an amulet because it is encased in leather. And so wrote the Radbaz (3:513), the Magen Avraham (43:14), the Erekh Leḥem (there), the Birkei Yosef (Yoreh De'ah 282:6), the Gedolei Ha-Kodesh (282:2) and the Mishna Berura (43:25). And so it is implied by the Rambam (Torah Scroll 10:5).<br>In practice, I wrote above that one must cover them with one cover, both because the prohibition is rabbinic and because this is the view of most poskim. In addition, according to many, when the book has a binding, the binding is considered one cover even with regard to marital relations (see Shiyurei Berakha, Oraḥ Ḥayim, Kuntres Aḥaron 154:1; Ḥesed La-Alafim, Oraḥ Ḥayim 240:8). However, some hold that it does not help as an additional cover for marital relations (Responsa Ohel Yosef 2; Peri Megadim 40, Eshel Avraham 2). And with printed books many are lenient with one cover (Kaf Ha-Ḥayim, Oraḥ Ḥayim 40:14; Darkhei Tahara 22:49). One may further add the view of some poskim who hold that if the books were printed without awareness that they are sacred books, they have no sanctity (Ḥavot Ya'ir 184; Maharsham 3:357. See below 11, note 21). And likewise one may be lenient with paper on which words of Torah were written, for although it lacks the lenient aspect of printing and binding, since it was written in a temporary manner and without names, one may make do with one cover (Tzitz Eliezer 11:5)."}]},{"slug":"21-09-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-12/","title_he":"יב – המשך","title_en":"12. Continued","body_en":"<p>Coins and banknotes on which verses are printed – have no sanctity, and it is permitted to enter the lavatory with them (Ḥavot Ya'ir 16; Mishna Berura 334:52; Yabi'a Omer 4:21; Tzitz Eliezer 16:31; below 11:9).</p>\n<p>One should not enter the lavatory with a bag, a shirt or a pendant on which there are three or more words of a verse. However, if he hides the pendant under his clothing, it is permitted to enter the lavatory with it. So that people will not stumble in this, it is right not to print parts of verses on shirts, since people enter the lavatory with them. However, when the words of a verse are used as a figure of speech without a meaning of sanctity, there is no prohibition. But generally the wording of the verse is used in order to give the message a sanctified value meaning, such as: \"Love your neighbour as yourself\", \"The land is exceedingly good\". And therefore one should avoid printing them on a shirt, and one should not enter the lavatory with them. However, in a pressing situation, one who is wearing such a shirt and has difficulty taking it off before he enters the lavatory may be lenient and enter with it. And there are verses about which it is doubtful whether they were brought with a meaning of sanctity or as a saying, such as \"Look at me and do likewise\", or \"Arise, walk through the land\". Regarding them it is good to be stringent, and one who wants to be lenient has grounds to rely on.<sup>16</sup></p>\n<p>It is permitted to read in the lavatory mundane books written in Hebrew (She'elat Ya'avetz 1:10; above 8, note 13). And likewise it is permitted to read a mundane newspaper in the lavatory (Igrot Moshe, Yoreh De'ah 2:70). And even when the newspaper has a few pages of words of Torah, as long as they are covered, because other pages are open – it is permitted to read the other pages. And there is no need to be concerned about bringing the newspaper into the lavatory, because the newspaper as a whole has not become sanctified on account of those pages of words of Torah, for most of it is mundane and consequently the aim of its printing is for mundane purposes (below 11:19).</p>\n<p>Out of honour for the sacred books, from the outset one should not change a baby's nappy in front of them (see Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 286:5; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav, Oraḥ Ḥayim 45:3). When necessary one may change a nappy in front of them, since the lack of respect is only for a brief moment, just as one who wears tefillin is permitted, when necessary, to pass through filthy alleys (Responsa Rema of Fano 59; Magen Avraham 43:11). And it is good that he separate with his back between the baby and the books, or turn the baby's head towards the books (the prohibition on marital relations in front of sacred books is explained in Peninei Halakha, Simḥat Ha-Bayit U-Virkhato 2:17).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":16,"en":"The Rambam wrote (Responsa 268) that one should not enter the lavatory with a tallit on which there are verses, but with what does not require scoring it is permitted. Regarding the obligation of scoring, we learned (above 8:10, note 11) that already three words of a verse have importance, and consequently one should not enter the lavatory with them. However, only when the three words were written for the purpose of study do they have importance, but one who weaves into his letter flourishes taken from verses, according to most poskim does not need to score, for they do not have the importance of a verse (Shulḥan Arukh 284:2; above 8, note 11). We also learned regarding coins on which holy names were stamped that since they did not intend the names to have sanctity, but only as a remembrance, they have no sanctity (Ḥavot Ya'ir 16). Accordingly, when three words of a verse were written on a shirt merely as a rhetorical saying, there is no prohibition on entering the lavatory in it. But many times there is uncertainty whether it is a rhetorical saying in the manner of those woven into a letter, or a verse intended to express a sanctified idea. In addition, some hold that even a rhetorical saying whose source is a verse is considered a verse (Shakh 284:2; above 8, note 11). Therefore, when it is clear that these are words of Torah, such as \"Love your neighbour as yourself\", or \"The land is exceedingly good\", it seems that one must be stringent (even if they are written in full spelling). And although we learned that according to many there is no sanctity in coins bearing names that were not stamped for the sake of sanctity, using them is not considered a degradation. Whereas here, where the writing is prominent, there is a degradation in entering the lavatory with words of a verse and a mitzva. Nevertheless, in a pressing situation, for example if he would be embarrassed to have to take off his shirt, one may combine the reasoning of the lenient views and enter the lavatory in it (see She'elat Shlomo 3:299). When it is less evident about the words that they are from a verse and a mitzva, such as: \"Look at me and do likewise\", or \"Arise, walk through the land\", it is good to be stringent, and one who is lenient has grounds to rely on."}]},{"slug":"21-09-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-13/","title_he":"יג – מהו ספר התורה המקודש","title_en":"13. What Is the Sanctified Torah Scroll","body_en":"<p>The sanctified Torah scroll is the scroll that was written in ink on parchment for the sake of the sanctity of a Torah scroll, and all the Divine Names in it were written for the sake of the sanctity of the Name. As it is said (Devarim 31:19): \"Now therefore write this song for yourselves\". 'Write for yourselves' – for the sake of the mitzva of writing the Torah scroll (Ramban; below 10:7, note 4).</p>\n<p>Some say that a single book of the Ḥumash written in ink on parchment for the sake of the sanctity of a Torah scroll is also holy with the sanctity of a Torah scroll, and must be honoured like a Torah scroll (Responsa Rashba 1:144). But in halakha it was ruled that although the single book is included among the sacred writings, and the level of its sanctity is above the books of the Prophets and the Writings, its sanctity is not like the sanctity of a Torah scroll, and the laws of honour towards a Torah scroll do not apply to it (Rambam, Torah Scroll 7:14; Beit Yosef, Yoreh De'ah 282:2; Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 283:1).<sup>17</sup></p>\n<p>If the Torah was written for its own sake and they erred and omitted one letter from it or added one letter to it, or omitted the space of an open or closed passage, or replaced the space of an open passage with a closed one or vice versa – the whole Torah scroll is invalid and has no sanctity of a Torah scroll (Rambam, Torah Scroll 7:11).</p>\n<p>If the invalid scroll wore out to the point that it cannot be repaired, it must be put away in a strict geniza in earthenware vessels (below 11:16). When it can be repaired, it should be kept until the repair in the Holy Ark together with the valid scrolls, just as in the Ark of the Covenant they placed beside the Tablets of the Covenant also the fragments of the first tablets (Sefer Ḥasidim 934). But a sign is made on the scroll so that people will know that it is invalid, for example by tying the binder over the mantle, as is the custom of those of Ashkenazi origin, or by tying a cloth at the top of the scroll, as is the custom of those of Sephardi origin. And since it has been marked as invalid, there is no obligation to repair it within thirty days.</p>\n<p>Some say that the laws of honouring a Torah scroll do not apply to an invalid scroll, and if it is carried one need not rise before it. And some say that the laws of honouring a Torah scroll apply to it, and therefore one must rise before it.<sup>18</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":17,"en":"According to Rabbi Yehuda, it is forbidden to join the sheets on which all the books of the Tanakh were written into one book; rather, the Torah should be separate, the books of the Prophets separate and the Writings separate. For one should not equate the sanctity of the Prophets and the Writings with the sanctity of the Torah, and just as it is forbidden to place the Prophets and the Writings on the Torah, so one should not join them together in a way that sometimes the sheets of the Prophets and the Writings will be on the sheets of the Torah. But the halakha was ruled according to the view of Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda Ha-Nasi, that it is permitted to join them together, except that since the Nakh was added to the Torah, it does not have the sanctity of a Torah scroll but the sanctity of other sacred books, and also one does not read from it in the public Torah reading (Bava Batra 13b; Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 283:1)."},{"n":18,"en":"We learned above in halakha 8, note 10, that as a rule it is forbidden to sell a Torah scroll, and even the seven leaders of the town are forbidden to sell it. But they are permitted to sell an invalid Torah scroll, for its law is like that of single books of the Ḥumash, which the seven leaders of the town may sell and use the proceeds for anything (the Rivash 285; Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 153:3). But if it was valid and became invalid, some say that its law is like a Torah scroll, which it is forbidden to sell (Kehillot Ya'akov 153:3; Da'at Torah 153:3). And some say that even in this case its law is like single books of the Ḥumash and it is permitted to sell it (Magen Avraham 153:3; Mishna Berura 8).<br>Some hold that the laws of honouring a Torah scroll do not apply to an invalid scroll, and therefore one need not stand before it, like the law of single books of the Ḥumash (Teshuva Me-Ahava 1:105; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 282:4). And some hold that as a rule it must be honoured like a valid scroll (Noda Bi-Yehuda, first edition, Yoreh De'ah 71; Ḥatam Sofer, Yoreh De'ah 2:279). And therefore one must stand before it (Masoret Moshe 4:274; Halikhot Shlomo, Prayer 12:44). And one fasts over its falling (Ḥayim Sha'al 1:12; Maharash Engel 6:10). As they said (Berakhot 8b): \"Be careful with an elder who forgot his learning through circumstances beyond his control, for we say: the Tablets and the fragments of the Tablets rest in the Ark\"."}]},{"slug":"21-09-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-09-14/","title_he":"יד – ספר תורה שנמצאה בו טעות","title_en":"14. A Torah Scroll in Which an Error Was Found","body_en":"<p>It is forbidden to keep a scroll that has an error in it for more than thirty days; rather, one must repair it, or mark it in order to repair it in the future, or put it in geniza, so that people will not come to err in words of Torah, as it is said (Iyov 11:14): \"and let not iniquity dwell in your tents\" (Ketubot 19b; Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 279:1).</p>\n<p>If three errors were found in a Torah scroll, it does not help to repair them alone; rather, one must check and proofread the whole scroll anew, for since three errors were found in it, its presumption of validity has been weakened, and one must check anew whether it was written according to halakha, and until then it is forbidden to read from it (Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 279:3).</p>\n<p>If an error was found in a scroll, one must hurry to repair it, lest people forget where the error was, and will have to proofread the whole scroll until they find the error and repair it. And as long as they have not found the error, the scroll is invalid.<sup>19</sup></p>\n<p>If a mishap occurred and the Torah scroll in which there was an error became mixed up among other scrolls – one must proofread all the scrolls thoroughly in order to find the error. And if they did not find it, after the fact one may read from all the scrolls (Ḥatam Sofer, Yoreh De'ah 277, based on the Beit Leḥem Yehuda).<sup>20</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":19,"en":"The Aḥaronim disagreed in two disputes regarding the law of a Torah scroll in which errors were found: <b>a)</b> Some say that the obligation to check the whole Torah scroll applies only if there were in it at one time three known errors, for example when each time an error was found they did not repair it, and so three errors accumulated. But if each time an error was found they repaired it immediately, since there were not three known errors in the scroll at one time – its presumption has not been weakened, and there is no obligation to check the whole of it. Only if in the end they found 85 errors in the scroll, even though there were not three errors at one time, must one check the whole of it (Benei Yona, Yoreh De'ah 279:7; Pitḥei Teshuva 279:7; Keset Ha-Sofer 19:2). And some say that even if each time an error was found they repaired it immediately, if three errors were found in the scroll – its presumption has been weakened, and one is obligated to check the whole of it (Eliya Rabba, Oraḥ Ḥayim 143:10; Davar Shmuel 117; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan, Yoreh De'ah 279:15). <b>b)</b> Some say that if it is known that the scroll was proofread by an expert proofreader, even if three errors were found in it at one and the same time, there is no obligation to proofread the whole of it, but they should repair the errors and the scroll is valid for reading (Keset Ha-Sofer 19:2). And some say that since three errors were found in it, one can no longer rely on the previous proofreading, and the whole of it must be proofread again (Davar Shmuel 117).<br>In practice, since some say that after the fact it is permitted to read from an invalid Torah scroll and to recite the blessing over it (Responsa of the Rambam 294; the Sages of Narbonne, the Ra'avya 553, and others; below 15:18), one can combine the two lines of reasoning, and then, if the scroll was properly proofread at first, and afterwards each time an error was found in it they repaired it immediately, so that there were not three errors in it at one and the same time, there is no need to proofread the whole of it (see Yeḥaveh Da'at 5:58). Therefore one must take care to repair every error immediately, so as not to enter into doubt regarding the validity of the Torah scroll. In any case, a Torah scroll in which several clear errors were found should rightly be proofread anew with a meticulous proofreading."},{"n":20,"en":"Even after they proofread thoroughly and did not find the error, the Ḥatam Sofer wrote (Yoreh De'ah 277) that one should not be lenient on the basis of 'nullification in the majority' among the other Torah scrolls, since the invalid Torah scroll is important and is not nullified; rather, one may be lenient on the basis of a combination of a 'double doubt' (sfek sfeika): a) perhaps there is no error in the Torah scroll from which we are now reading; b) even if there is an error in it, perhaps the error is in another book of the Ḥumash, and according to the Ran and those who agree with him it is permitted to read from a book of the Ḥumash in which there is no error. And one may add a third doubt, that even if the halakha is not like the Ran, perhaps the halakha follows the view of those who hold that after the fact one may read with a blessing from an invalid scroll (below 15:18, note 18)."}]}]});
